  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Electric Boat obligated $8.6B in federal contract dollars in FY2026 to date. Electric Boat recorded 263 awards this fiscal year to date, with an average award size of $32.7M. Obligations fell 59.8% year over year. Fiscal-year obligations have varied: FY21 totaled $8.7B, FY22 totaled $9.4B, FY23 totaled $10.8B, FY24 totaled $8.6B, FY25 totaled $21.4B, and FY26 totaled $8.6B to date. Engineering Services is the largest NAICS market for Electric Boat in FY2026 to date, with $6.9B in obligations, followed by Ship Building and Repairing with $1.7B. Smaller manufacturing and R&D NAICS categories also appear in the top 10 markets, including Instruments and Related Products Manufacturing for Measuring, Displaying, and Controlling Industrial Process Variables at $2.4M and Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences at $1.6M. The current-year totals are partial and reflect fiscal year to date obligations. This snapshot highlights contract scale, recent year-to-year change, and the principal NAICS markets that account for Electric Boat’s federal work.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What explains the drop in obligations year over year?",
        "body": "Obligations fell 59.8% year over year. Fiscal-year totals show large swings, with FY25 at $21.4B and FY26 at $8.6B to date, indicating year-to-year variability in award timing or contract phasing. Electric Boat still recorded 263 awards in FY2026 to date, with an average award size of $32.7M, so activity continues even with lower total obligations."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Where are most of Electric Boat’s federal dollars concentrated?",
        "body": "Engineering Services is the dominant market with $6.9B in obligations in FY2026 to date. Ship Building and Repairing is the second-largest market with $1.7B. Several smaller manufacturing and R&D NAICS categories round out the top 10, including a $2.4M market for instruments manufacturing and a $1.6M market for physical and engineering R&D."
      }
    ]
  },
